# Name: ZX Spectrum
#
# Sinclair ZX Spectrum character set - the only three bytes that differ
# from plain US-ASCII in the printable 0x20-0x7F range.
# Source: Wikipedia "ZX Spectrum character set" (quoted directly: "code
# point 0x5E is an up-arrow (up arrow) instead of a caret (^), 0x60 is
# the pound sign (GBP) instead of the grave accent (`), and 0x7F is the
# copyright sign (C) instead of the control character DEL").
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ZX_Spectrum_character_set
#
# The entire 0x80-0xFF range maps to U+FFFD (REPLACEMENT CHARACTER)
# rather than a real glyph, for three different underlying reasons (same
# "leave out rather than guess" policy as the built-in CP437/PETSCII
# charsets, see TODO.md Abschnitt 6.3):
# - 0x80-0x8F: the 16 iconic 2x2 block-graphics characters. Their
#   general existence and Unicode Block Elements equivalents are well
#   documented, but every source found (Wikipedia, the official
#   Sinclair BASIC manual's own character-set appendix, ROM/font
#   ripper projects) shows the exact byte-to-quadrant assignment only
#   as an image, never as text/a table of code points - nothing
#   verifiable enough to transcribe with confidence instead of
#   guessing a plausible-looking but possibly mirrored/rotated order.
# - 0x90-0xA4: User-Defined Graphics (UDG) - by design there is no
#   universal glyph here at all; real Spectrum software POKEs its own
#   bitmap into this range, so no single correct mapping could exist
#   even in principle.
# - 0xA5-0xFF: tokenized BASIC keywords (PRINT, SAVE, LOAD, ...). Not
#   representable in this file format at all - each entry here is one
#   byte to exactly one display character, but a BASIC token byte
#   stands for a whole keyword (multiple characters), not a single
#   glyph.
# All three cases are mapped to U+FFFD instead of left unlisted, on
# purpose: an unlisted byte falls back to plain identity, which would
# display as an invisible control-picture character - silently implying
# nothing special is there, when a real Spectrum genuinely shows
# *something* (a graphic, a user-defined glyph, a whole keyword) at
# every one of these positions (Codex review finding, applied here even
# though none of these three ranges hit the format's astral-codepoint
# limit specifically). Expect the app's own duplicate-mapping warning
# for this whole range when this file loads - harmless and expected,
# since all 128 bytes intentionally share one target code point.
5E=2191
60=00A3
7F=00A9
80=FFFD
81=FFFD
